node_modules/
# Released agent artifacts, produced by `npm run build:agent`.
#
# THE BINARIES DO NOT BELONG IN GIT — four ~8MB files per release, and git keeps a blob
# forever. Nothing needs them here either: the image builds its own in the Dockerfile's
# `agent` stage and copies them in AFTER `COPY . .` (so a committed one would be
# overwritten), CI builds its own to check the manifest against, and `.dockerignore`
# excludes them from the build context for the same reason.
#
# THE MANIFEST AND THE CHECKSUMS DO — they are a few KB of text, and they are the record
# of what was published: which four artifacts, at which version, with which sha256. That
# is what lets somebody verify a binary they were handed, and what makes a rebuild
# checkable rather than merely repeatable. The build is deliberately reproducible
# (`-trimpath`, `-buildvcs=false`, Go pinned by `agent/go.mod`), so a rebuild that
# disagrees with a committed hash is a finding rather than noise.
#
# ⚠ DENY FIRST, THEN ALLOW, and the bare `**/` line is load-bearing: git will not look
# inside an excluded directory, so without re-including the directories themselves the
# two allow rules below can never match. Written this way round on purpose — a new kind
# of build output lands ignored unless somebody decides otherwise.
apps/web/static/agent/**
!apps/web/static/agent/**/
!apps/web/static/agent/**/manifest.json
!apps/web/static/agent/**/SHA256SUMS
output/lighthouse/
playwright-report/
playwright/.auth/
test-results/
tests/playwright-report/
tests/playwright/
tests/test-results/

# ⚠ THE DEPLOYMENT PROFILE IS NOT PUBLIC. It names the docker context, the SSH
# endpoint fingerprint, the private address it binds to and the host paths of the
# env files — a map of somebody's internal network, in a repository anybody can
# read. It lives with the deployment record instead and is copied in when a deploy
# runs. `publish.json` is the tracked one: image repositories and nothing else.
.podokit/deploy/*.json
!.podokit/deploy/publish.json
